{"timestamp":"2026-04-14T19:53:43.014Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"The key distinction is between:\n\n- writing the attached session to v2\n- writing the full checkpoint state to v2\n\n`attach` only has one new session in hand. If it simply dual-writes that one session to v2, that is not enough when the checkpoint ID already existed in v1.\n\nWhy:\n\n1. `attach` reuses the existing checkpoint ID from `HEAD` if there is already an `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer. See [attach.go](/Users/ninawork/entire/devenv/cli/cmd/entire/cli/attach.go:284).\n2. v1 `WriteCommitted` merges that session into the existing v1 checkpoint.\n3. v2 `WriteCommitted` can only merge against what already exists in v2, not what exists in v1.\n\nThat last part is the whole issue.\n\n`V2GitStore.WriteCommitted` only reads the existing summary from the v2 `/main` ref. If the checkpoint does not already exist in v2, it creates a fresh checkpoint with just the session being written. See [v2_committed.go](/Users/ninawork/entire/devenv/cli/cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/v2_committed.go:274) and [v2_committed.go](/Users/ninawork/entire/devenv/cli/cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/v2_committed.go:302).\n\nSo the problematic case is:\n\n**Before attach**\n- v1 checkpoint `X` exists with session `A`\n- v2 checkpoint `X` does not exist yet\n- `HEAD` already has `Entire-Checkpoint: X`\n\n**Then attach session `B` with v2 enabled**\n- v1 write updates checkpoint `X` to contain `A + B`\n- naive v2 write creates checkpoint `X` with only `B`, because v2 had no prior copy of `X`\n\n**After attach without backfill**\n- v1 `X` = `A + B`\n- v2 `X` = `B` only\n\nThat is why “write to v1 and v2” is not sufficient by itself. If you only write the attached session to both places, the two stores diverge whenever the checkpoint already existed only in v1.\n\nSo:\n\n- If by “write it to v1 and v2” you mean “write only the newly attached session”, backfill is necessary.\n- If by “write it to v1 and v2” you mean “make both stores contain the entire checkpoint after attach”, then yes, that is exactly what `backfillAttachCheckpointToV2` is doing.\n\nA minimal reproduction:\n\n1. Disable `checkpoints_v2`\n2. Create checkpoint `X` on `HEAD` from session `A`\n3. Enable `checkpoints_v2`\n4. Run `entire attach` for session `B` on that same `HEAD`\n\nWithout backfill:\n- v1: `X = [A, B]`\n- v2: `X = [B]`\n\nWith backfill:\n- v1: `X = [A, B]`\n- v2: `X = [A, B]`\n\nThat’s the case the helper solves.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
{"timestamp":"2026-04-14T19:53:43.014Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"The key distinction is between:\n\n- writing the attached session to v2\n- writing the full checkpoint state to v2\n\n`attach` only has one new session in hand. If it simply dual-writes that one session to v2, that is not enough when the checkpoint ID already existed in v1.\n\nWhy:\n\n1. `attach` reuses the existing checkpoint ID from `HEAD` if there is already an `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer. See [attach.go](/Users/ninawork/entire/devenv/cli/cmd/entire/cli/attach.go:284).\n2. v1 `WriteCommitted` merges that session into the existing v1 checkpoint.\n3. v2 `WriteCommitted` can only merge against what already exists in v2, not what exists in v1.\n\nThat last part is the whole issue.\n\n`V2GitStore.WriteCommitted` only reads the existing summary from the v2 `/main` ref. If the checkpoint does not already exist in v2, it creates a fresh checkpoint with just the session being written. See [v2_committed.go](/Users/ninawork/entire/devenv/cli/cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/v2_committed.go:274) and [v2_committed.go](/Users/ninawork/entire/devenv/cli/cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/v2_committed.go:302).\n\nSo the problematic case is:\n\n**Before attach**\n- v1 checkpoint `X` exists with session `A`\n- v2 checkpoint `X` does not exist yet\n- `HEAD` already has `Entire-Checkpoint: X`\n\n**Then attach session `B` with v2 enabled**\n- v1 write updates checkpoint `X` to contain `A + B`\n- naive v2 write creates checkpoint `X` with only `B`, because v2 had no prior copy of `X`\n\n**After attach without backfill**\n- v1 `X` = `A + B`\n- v2 `X` = `B` only\n\nThat is why “write to v1 and v2” is not sufficient by itself. If you only write the attached session to both places, the two stores diverge whenever the checkpoint already existed only in v1.\n\nSo:\n\n- If by “write it to v1 and v2” you mean “write only the newly attached session”, backfill is necessary.\n- If by “write it to v1 and v2” you mean “make both stores contain the entire checkpoint after attach”, then yes, that is exactly what `backfillAttachCheckpointToV2` is doing.\n\nA minimal reproduction:\n\n1. Disable `checkpoints_v2`\n2. Create checkpoint `X` on `HEAD` from session `A`\n3. Enable `checkpoints_v2`\n4. Run `entire attach` for session `B` on that same `HEAD`\n\nWithout backfill:\n- v1: `X = [A, B]`\n- v2: `X = [B]`\n\nWith backfill:\n- v1: `X = [A, B]`\n- v2: `X = [A, B]`\n\nThat’s the case the helper solves."}],"phase":"final_answer"}}
